NUM-19: book an outfall's net flow when it both receives and sends flow

node_getSystemOutflow() returned the outfall's inflow when it had no
outflow and minus its outflow when it had no inflow, but 0 when it had both,
so removeOutflows() booked nothing for it. Two ordinary cases give both: a
FLOW (or DWF, RDII, ...) inflow at an outfall that feeds the network, and
every SKIP_STEADY_STATE step that follows a step on which the function set
a feeding outfall's inflow to its outflow for reporting. Return the net
flow, inflow - outflow, which is what the other two cases already return;
remember (Outfall.inflowIsBackflow) when the inflow was set for reporting,
and on a skipped step put it back to 0 before quality routing and
removeOutflows() see it. Results only change for outfalls that both
receive and send flow, and on skipped steps that follow one.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-19_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/node.c b/src/legacy/engine/node.c
index 070db5a..898260a 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/node.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/node.c
@@ -431,6 +431,8 @@ double node_getSystemOutflow(int j, int *isFlooded)
     // --- if node is an outfall
     if ( Node[j].type == OUTFALL )
     {
+        Outfall[Node[j].subIndex].inflowIsBackflow = FALSE;
+
         // --- node receives inflow from outfall conduit
         if ( Node[j].outflow == 0.0 ) outflow = Node[j].inflow;
 
@@ -438,10 +440,12 @@ double node_getSystemOutflow(int j, int *isFlooded)
         //     (therefore it has a negative outflow)
         else
         {
+            // --- net of any inflow the outfall also receives
+            outflow = Node[j].inflow - Node[j].outflow;
             if ( Node[j].inflow == 0.0 )
             {
-                outflow = -Node[j].outflow;
                 Node[j].inflow = fabs(outflow);
+                Outfall[Node[j].subIndex].inflowIsBackflow = TRUE;
             }
         }
 
